Root cause:
The bug is a memory leak of per-CPU net_dev structures allocated in dpaa2_dpseci_setup. When commit 0e1a4d427f58 converted the net_device from embedded to dynamically allocated pointers, the cleanup for these allocations was only added in dpaa2_dpseci_disable (called during normal shutdown), but was missing in dpaa2_dpseci_free, which is called in error paths (e.g., when dpaa2_dpseci_dpio_setup fails). Additionally, the local clean_mask used to track allocated netdevs was not stored in the private structure, so it could not be used later for cleanup.

Code statements information:
The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
// In dpaa2_dpseci_setup: local clean_mask not stored in priv
if (!zalloc_cpumask_var(&clean_mask, GFP_KERNEL))

// In dpaa2_dpseci_disable: netdev freed only on normal shutdown, not in error paths
free_netdev(ppriv->net_dev);

// In dpaa2_dpseci_free: no cleanup of netdevs (missing loop)
```
Reason:
The conversion to dynamically allocated net_dev added cleanup in `dpaa2_dpseci_disable` (normal shutdown) but not in `dpaa2_dpseci_free` (error path). Additionally, the local `clean_mask` was not stored in the private structure, so `dpaa2_dpseci_free` could not determine which CPUs had allocated netdevs to free them. This caused memory leaks when probe failed after setup.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
// In dpaa2_dpseci_setup: store clean_mask in priv
if (!zalloc_cpumask_var(&priv->clean_mask, GFP_KERNEL))

// In dpaa2_dpseci_free: free netdevs using stored clean_mask
for_each_cpu(i, priv->clean_mask) {
	ppriv = per_cpu_ptr(priv->ppriv, i);
	free_netdev(ppriv->net_dev);
}
free_cpumask_var(priv->clean_mask);

// In dpaa2_dpseci_disable: remove the now-redundant free_netdev call
// (the line is deleted)
```
Reason:
The fix moves netdev cleanup from `dpaa2_dpseci_disable` to `dpaa2_dpseci_free` and stores the CPU mask of allocated netdevs in `priv->clean_mask`. This ensures that all per-CPU netdevs are freed during error paths (e.g., when `dpaa2_dpseci_dpio_setup` fails) as well as during normal shutdown, preventing memory leaks.


